    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-14656",
      "problem": "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25?$\n\nI. $7$\n\nII. $10$\n\nIII. $12$",
      "worked_solution": "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $7$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(7,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $10$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(10,25)=5\\neq1. \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- $12$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(12,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
